{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Then Jacob was greatly afraid</b> — because they told him that Esau had gone out from his city and was coming to meet Jacob, and further that he had taken with him many men, four hundred; he was greatly afraid for his own life, for he said, \"He would not have taken all these except to make war against me.\" Now what appears to me regarding this matter is that Esau did not receive the emissaries fittingly and paid no attention to them — perhaps they were not even brought before him, for he did not give permission for them to come into his presence and speak with him at all. For Scripture relates that he asked them, \"What is the welfare of my brother, and what is his situation, and the situation of his household and his children?\" And they called him by name and said to him that Jacob was coming to meet him, to see him, and they reported this to Jacob. But Scripture does not relate that the emissaries said anything in Esau's name; rather, his wrath was stored up in his heart, and to do him harm he was marching with this armed force. And behold, the emissaries investigated in the camp and knew that he was coming to meet Jacob. And this is the meaning of \"and also (וגם)\": for they said, \"We came to your brother, to Esau,\" and he answered us nothing and sent you no words of peace, \"and also he is coming to meet you\" with force and an armed host. Therefore fear was added upon his fear: \"Then Jacob was greatly afraid and it distressed him.\" And so our Rabbis said (Bereshit Rabbah 75:7) that the emissaries recognized hatred in him; they said, \"We came to your brother, to Esau\" — you conduct yourself toward him as a brother, but he conducts himself toward you as Esau. But in the end, when he saw the great honor that Jacob showed him, and that he humbled himself before him by bowing down to the ground seven times from a distance until he drew near to him, his compassion was stirred, and he thought that he was acknowledging the birthright and his superiority over him, as I have explained (Ramban on Genesis 32:5), and he was reconciled by this — for the hearts are HaShem's; toward whatever He desires He inclines them."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {0 "Ramban's midrash citation '(ב\"ר עה ז)' refers to Bereshit Rabbah 75:7."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:38:24.061506371Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}